{"tag":"JX0F","kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"prop-1","file":"","label":"def","number":"1","section":"sec-1","order":1,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"\\label{def}\nThere is a canonical construction of a generalized function $\\delta(f)$ such that for any $(n-1)$-form form $\\nu$ on a neighborhood of $Y$ in $M$ and any smooth function $g$ we have\n\\begin{equation}\n\\int_M\\delta(f)g\\,df\\wedge\\nu=\\int_Y g\\nu\n\\end{equation}","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"prop","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"prop","number_from":"render","labels":["def"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"cited","origin":"cited","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":77,"to":82},"hash":"sha256:3b7bbbc261ec65c8fd07421e5573461ce43de5024467b5b8819103ee3aa4dcfc","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"GJPG","kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"prop-2","file":"","label":"local","number":"2","section":"sec-1","order":2,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"\\label{local}\n Given local coordinates $(x_1,\\cdots,x_n)$ on an open subset $U$ of $M$ such that $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1}\\neq 0$ on $U$, we have\n\n \\begin{equation}\n \\int_M \\delta(f)\\, |dx_1\\cdots dx_n|\n = \\int_Y \\left|\\left(\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1}\\right)\\right|^{-1}\\, |dx_2\\cdots dx_n|\\Big\\rvert_Y.\n \\end{equation}","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"prop","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"prop","number_from":"render","labels":["local"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":102,"to":109},"hash":"sha256:e77383b9cab63fd1cab3666e350001fe9d97b94bb5c9a118460e5c12ffb927b0","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-2","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"LRYM","kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"prop-3","file":"","number":"3","section":"sec-2","order":3,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"We have the equality between generalized functions over $\\mathbb{R}$\n\\begin{equation}\n\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}\\, \\delta(x-a)\\vert da\\vert =1\n    \\end{equation}\n  Here $\\delta(x-a)$ is the generalized function which  evaluates to $f(a)$ when evaluated  against a smooth compactly density $f(x)\\vert dx\\vert$.  Equivalently, translations on $\\mathbb{R}$ act naturally on smooth compactly supported  densities, hence dually on generalized functions.\n  \n  The integral above is understood as a weak integral (aka Pettis integral).","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"prop","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"prop","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":141,"to":149},"hash":"sha256:73d0b061cf755dad4b0e9899d218251f6243593df832d531e3add7a512dee613","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-3","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"JZS4","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"proof-1","file":"","section":"sec-2","order":4,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Since the integral of generalized funcions  in question is a weak (aka Pettis) integral, we compute it against a test smooth density $g(x)\\,|dx|$:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}\\!\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}\\delta(x-a)\\,|da|\\right) g(x)\\,|dx|\n=\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}\\!|da|\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}\\delta(x-a)\\,g(x)\\,|dx|\n=\\int_{\\mathbb{R}} g(a)\\,|da|.\n\\end{equation}\nSince this equals $\\int_{\\mathbb{R}} g(x)\\,|dx|$ for all $g$, we have $\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}\\delta(x-a)\\,|da|=1$ as generalized functions.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"prop-3","attach":3,"span":{"file":"main.tex","from":151,"to":159},"hash":"sha256:4e7bcf6092d7226790b8940ef56678407b451d706870b96c89372393f8d09a58","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#proof-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"WKVY","kind":"definition","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"definition-1","file":"","number":"3.1","section":"sec-3","order":5,"title":"Definition","body_md":"A  generalized function $g$ on a smooth manifold $M$ is a continuous functional on the space $Dens_c(M)$ of smooth compactly supported densities on $M$., where $Dens_c(M)$ is vieweed a the direct limit of the Frechet spaces $Dens_K(M)$ of smooth densities supportee on compact subsets $K$ of $M$. The space of generalized functions on $M$ is denoted by $\\mathcal D^{\\prime}(M)$","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"definition","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"definition","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":165,"to":168},"hash":"sha256:dd4bc45cc8de2a5ef4d7a9ff2cc5b2bb65c28fbe6524f3860f9be5ca25716256","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-4","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"KNS2","kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"prop-4","file":"","number":"4","section":"sec-3","order":6,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"If the function $f$  has nowhere vanishing differentiaL  $df$, there is a well-define fiber integration map $\\int_f::Dens_c(M)\\to Dens_c(\\mathbb{R})$.\nThen the pull-back map $f^*: \\mathcal D^{\\prime}(\\mathbb{R})\\to \\mathcal D^{\\prime}(M)$ is well-defined and continuous.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"prop","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"prop","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":177,"to":181},"hash":"sha256:0d9c9d18d6467e53d38b09828b55c5d158754a1cad26cd5744fafd33489478ae","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-5","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"8GTA","kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"prop-5","file":"","number":"5","section":"sec-3","order":7,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"Under the assumption that $df$ vanishes nowhere on the locus $Y=f^{-1}(0)$, we can define $\\delta(f)=f^*\\delta(x)$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"prop","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"prop","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":185,"to":189},"hash":"sha256:304bcf3083480357c1ee4820b480c6d77fb8a2e8516ee64fca91be1d855b561d","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-6","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"QANG","kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"prop-6","file":"","number":"6","section":"sec-3","order":8,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"We have $\\int_M\\delta(f)g\\omega =\\int_Y\\, \\vert\\vert \\nabla f\\vert\\vert^{-1} g\\nu$","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"prop","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"prop","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":208,"to":211},"hash":"sha256:1f8319a59b95e4aa50d1e0398ab3301ec88951b23b2ba0aae961009f7b687423","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-7","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"3FCP","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"proof-2","file":"","section":"sec-3","order":9,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Working on a local tubular neighborhood $T$ of $Y$ in $M$, we have local coordinates $(x_1,\\cdots,x_{n-1},r)$ where $r$ is the radial coordinate perpendicular to $Y$. Then on $Y$ itself (but not on a neighborhood of $Y$) we have $\\vert\\omega\\vert=\\vert \\nu\\cdot dr\\vert$. Also on $Y$ we have $df=d(f_{/Y})+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial r}dr$, so that $\\bigl(\\frac{\\omega}{df}\\bigr)_{Y}=(\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial r})^{-1}\\nu=\\lVert \\nabla f\\rVert^{-1} \\nu$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"prop-6","attach":3,"span":{"file":"main.tex","from":212,"to":214},"hash":"sha256:a3559a1ec43266432862cd118d53b3425ca8b1dcbf76f2b24ad9875c100e5763","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#proof-2","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"1W08","kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"prop-7","file":"","label":"tot2","number":"7","section":"sec-3","order":10,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"\\label{tot2}\nIf the mapping $f:M\\to\\mathbb{R}$ has non-zero differential at each point of $M$, we have the equation\n\\begin{equation}\n\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}\\, \\delta(f-a) da =1\n\\end{equation}","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"prop","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"prop","number_from":"render","labels":["tot2"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":218,"to":223},"hash":"sha256:75568d21073b9dbffc90a633767062d3a6ad8756dfd8842606657bd3f4be3368","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-8","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"43FV","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"proof-3","file":"","section":"sec-3","order":11,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Apply the pullback map  $f^*$ to the equation $\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}\\, \\delta(x-a)da=1$, noting that the pull-back map respects weak integrals.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"prop-7","attach":3,"span":{"file":"main.tex","from":225,"to":227},"hash":"sha256:ad182ba5862e1ebaa950e02d5ad9f5129ca1621464aeaaf6f99a691710b02048","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#proof-3","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"BD56","kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"prop-8","file":"","number":"8","section":"sec-4","order":12,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"Let $(f_1,\\cdots,f_k)$ be smooth functions on a smooth manifold $M$ such that the smooth mapping $F=(f_1,\\cdots,f_k):M\\to\\mathbb{R}^k$ is a submersion at all points of $Y=F^{-1}(0)$, then the pullback \n$F^*(\\delta(x_1)\\cdots\\delta(x_k))$  is well-defined, and will be denoted $\\delta(f_1)\\cdots\\delta(f_k)$. This product of delta functions is commutative and associative under the submersive assumptions.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"prop","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"prop","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":241,"to":245},"hash":"sha256:c37647fb2a260322f3a033c6dd5eb70b280d628f92ae400aefb40557f2196c7d","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-9","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"S6FQ","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"proof-4","file":"","section":"sec-4","order":13,"title":"Proof","body_md":"When $F$ is a submersion, the fiber integration map\n  $\\int_F::Dens_c(M)\\to Dens_c(\\mathbb{R}^k)$ is well-defined and is continuous for the inductive limit Fr\\'echet topologies. The dual map $F^*$ is a continuous map from generalized functions on $\\mathbb{R}^k$ to generalized functions on $M$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"prop-8","attach":3,"span":{"file":"main.tex","from":247,"to":251},"hash":"sha256:651fc6036c15f2d77c4be85fd0fde0e520f71734a8815ce89662f39c4ba9c2ad","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#proof-4","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"ASUZ","kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"prop-9","file":"","number":"9","section":"sec-4","order":14,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"If $(g_1,\\cdots,g_k)$ are a new set of variables defining $Y$, then we have\n\\begin{equation}\n\\delta(g_1)\\cdots\\delta(g_k)=\\vert J\\vert^{-1}\\, \\delta(f_1)\\cdots\\delta(f_k)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $J=\\det\\left(\\frac{\\partial g_i}{\\partial f_j}\\right)$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"prop","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"prop","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":266,"to":272},"hash":"sha256:32020f09fc4ca98497474e985e241e0da677464ec7c87f941ec12f92535b12c5","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-10","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"1H3N","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"proof-5","file":"","section":"sec-4","order":15,"title":"Proof","body_md":"We have the equation $dg_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dg_k=J\\, df_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge df_k$ which yields immediately\n\\begin{equation}\n \\frac{\\omega}{dg_1\\cdots dg_k}=J^{-1}\\frac{\\omega}{df_1\\cdots df_k}.\n\\end{equation}","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"prop-9","attach":3,"span":{"file":"main.tex","from":274,"to":279},"hash":"sha256:2fdd374727d0cc4785ad0e5c6b1ef1aa805d21184bf2f1857076ecdc1f0a70c7","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#proof-5","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"XEV3","kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"prop-10","file":"","label":"local","number":"10","section":"sec-4","order":16,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"\\label{local}\n\n\\begin{equation}\n\\int_M\\, \\delta(f_1)\\cdots \\delta(f_k)\\, g\\,|\\omega|\n=\\int_Y\\, |NJ(F)|^{-1}\\, g\\, \\vert\\nu\\vert.\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $NJ(F)$ is the \\underline{normal jacobian} of $F$, i.e., the determinant of the restriction of the differential of $F$ to the normal bundle of $Y$. See e.g. \\cite{Ch} for the normal jacobian.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"prop","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"prop","number_from":"render","labels":["local"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":283,"to":290},"hash":"sha256:dc2420a2841296a9e41a2e7270eb840f3cc6d7970f70c9cafdf48d3df3684d65","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-11","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"1IIX","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"proof-6","file":"","section":"sec-4","order":17,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Fix $y\\in Y$ and consideer  the orthogonal decomposition $T_yM=T_yY\\oplus N_y$ with $\\dim N_y=k$. Let $\\xi_1,\\dots,\\xi_k$ be an orthornormal basis of $N_y^*$ such that\n$df_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge df_k = NJ(F)(y)\\,\\xi_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge\\xi_k$ on $N_y$. Contracting the volume density $\\vert\\omega\\vert$ by $|df_1,\\dots,df_k|$ therefore produces the induced riemannian density on $T_yY$ scaled by $NJ(F)(y)^{-1}$, i.e. $NJ(F)^{-1}\\vert\\nu\\vert$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"prop-10","attach":3,"span":{"file":"main.tex","from":292,"to":295},"hash":"sha256:eb4565f650d90f1143dd008056ffd0b21f94d73ff71a05236ba7926ec8224847","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#proof-6","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"3POA","kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"prop-11","file":"","label":"total","number":"11","section":"sec-4","order":18,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"\\label{total}\nIf the mapping $F:M\\to \\mathbb{R}^k$ is a submersion, we have\n\\begin{equation}\n\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^k}\\, \\delta(f_1-a_1)\\cdots \\delta(f_k-a_k)\\,\\vert da_1\\cdots da_k\\vert=1.\n\\end{equation}","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"prop","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"prop","number_from":"render","labels":["total"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":299,"to":304},"hash":"sha256:b5cee4a165719064831d1a7a8cb6d43bd6aa3a5f122bca1756c277e10f7656c1","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-12","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"Q6MM","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"theorem-1","file":"","number":"4.1","section":"sec-4","order":19,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"We have the following smooth coarea formula\n\\begin{equation}\n\\mathop{\\int}_{\\mathbb{R}^k}\\, g\\omega=\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^k}\\, \n    |da_1\\cdots da_k|\\bigl(\\mathop{\\int}_{F^{-1}(\\bar{a} )}|NJ(F)|^{-1}g\n\\nu_{\\bar{a}}\\bigr),\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\bar a=(a_1,\\cdots,a_k)$ and $\\nu_{\\bar a}$ is the riemannian volume form on $F^{-1}(\\bar a)$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"theorem","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":307,"to":315},"hash":"sha256:bf82416d0da5fda7d31aa2a1c6a18800a51f2de8c88f3ae3217719ad3c2a4d9a","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-13","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"S31F","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"proof-7","file":"","section":"sec-4","order":20,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Combine the local coarea formula \\ref{local} with the total area property \\ref{total}","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"theorem-1","attach":3,"span":{"file":"main.tex","from":318,"to":320},"hash":"sha256:baabc6e68d28d26344b9f6cb2859aa897788d0cd1d4eb59012730de664d27e12","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#proof-7","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"BABE","kind":"statement","subkind":"proposition","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"prop-12","file":"","number":"12","section":"sec-5","order":21,"title":"Proposition","body_md":"Assume that the mapping $F:M\\to\\mathbb{R}^k$ is submersion. Then the marginal probability distribution of the $f_i$'s has a smooth pdf $g(\\bar a)$ (probability density function) given by $g(\\bar a)=\\int_M, \\delta(f_1-a_1)\\cdots\\delta(f_k-a_k)|\\omega|$","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"prop","item":"statement/proposition","head":"environment","counter":"prop","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":328,"to":329},"hash":"sha256:457c8a0b858e3534a4fe2b8d23113be1bcee882a3a6de27186a5314b56d23b39","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-14","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"1N7H","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"proof-8","file":"","section":"sec-5","order":22,"title":"Proof","body_md":"We have\n\\begin{align*}\n\\int_M\\, \\delta(f_1-a_1)\\cdots\\delta(f_k-a_k)|\\omega\u0026|=\\int_M\\, F^*\\bigl(\\delta)(x_1-a_1)\\cdots\\delta(x_k-a_k)|\\omega|\\\\\n\u0026= \\int_M\\, \\delta(x_1-a_1)\\cdots \\delta(x_k-a_k)F_*|\\omega|\\\\\n\u0026=g(\\bar a)\n    \\end{align*}","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"prop-12","attach":3,"span":{"file":"main.tex","from":331,"to":339},"hash":"sha256:230c204a4d0b6b4ea3b8f4fb088d86f56ce99988cc27c21070200db2109f6ac0","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#proof-8","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"CSHJ","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"lemma-1","file":"","number":"5.1","section":"sec-5","order":23,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"The mapping $F:S^{n-1}\\to \\mathbb{R}^k$ given by $F(x)=(x_1,\\cdots,x_k)$ is a submersion.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":344,"to":346},"hash":"sha256:9d9d265c335f2bff89f96273d9b640348283cae4e497683cd916f7a3e480f3e3","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-15","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"DWNV","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"proof-9","file":"","section":"sec-5","order":24,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Consider the mapping $h:\\mathbb{R}^n\\to \\mathbb{R}^{k+1}$ given by\n\\[h(x)=(x_1,\\dots,x_k,\\,x_1^2+\\cdots+x_n^2).\\]\nIt is a submersion since the differentials of these $k+1$ functions are linearly independent everywhere. Restricting $h$ to the level set $x_1^2+\\cdots+x_n^2=1$ shows that $F$ has rank $k$ on $S^{n-1}$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"lemma-1","attach":3,"span":{"file":"main.tex","from":348,"to":352},"hash":"sha256:6a610e198185040066d0cfc8b37981f487db3e883158216e1cd4b579822c2f37","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#proof-9","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"GUWZ","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"lemma-2","file":"","number":"5.2","section":"sec-5","order":25,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"The invariant volume density $|\\omega|$ on $S^{n-1}$ satisfies\n\\begin{equation}\n|\\omega|=2\\,\\frac{|dx|}{d(r^2)},\\qquad r^2=\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2.\n\\end{equation}","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main.tex","from":356,"to":361},"hash":"sha256:45c0ccef4ec006ad6db4b815ac181d736112c78b4136be8797ec9d9fbf171de2","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#env-16","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
{"tag":"T53P","kind":"proof","paper":"2610.18922","version":"1","local":"proof-10","file":"","section":"sec-5","order":26,"title":"Proof","body_md":"In polar coordinates, $|dx|=r^{n-1}|\\omega|\\,|dr|$, hence $|dx|/|dr|=r^{n-1}|\\omega|$. On the locus $r=1$ we have $d(r^2)=2r\\,dr=2\\,dr$, giving the stated identity.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"lemma-2","attach":3,"span":{"file":"main.tex","from":363,"to":365},"hash":"sha256:e778022d360c1f7bd6ee87e28d0f93d78926def25fee9525ab8363c55f0b301b","minhash":"","census":"2610.18922v1#proof-10","extractor":"ax/v0.5.28"}
